WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.800
Remediation guidance should be clear enough for developers to act on.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:11.780
A good finding explains the affected code context, why the pattern is risky, what safer pattern is expected, and how the fix can be validated.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.080
Generic warnings create friction.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:23.260
Practical guidance turns scanner output into learning, especially when it links to team-approved libraries, examples, secure coding standards, or tests.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:35.800
Common remediation themes include parameterized APIs, output encoding, input validation, secrets removal, safer library choices, secure configuration, and appropriate access checks.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.180
These controls solve different problems.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.000
Parameterized APIs prevent data from changing query structure.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:46.060
Context-aware encoding prevents output from becoming executable content.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.480
Input validation enforces expected shape and values.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.140
Secrets removal eliminates credentials from code and artifacts.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.120
Fix validation matters.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:01:01.580
A superficial change may silence the tool without reducing risk, or it may move the problem to another code path.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:10.440
Teams should add unit or integration tests for security behavior where practical, rerun the relevant scan, and review the change in context.

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.820
The goal is not merely a clean report.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.000
The goal is a corrected implementation that preserves the intended security behavior.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:22.060
SAST feedback should teach repeatable secure coding habits.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:30.840
If a finding recurs across teams, the organization may need a helper function, framework wrapper, standard pattern, training example, or design change.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:37.300
Developer workflow improves when feedback is timely, specific, and connected to the tools developers already use.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.320
Remediation is most durable when it improves the system and the team’s shared practice.
